    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d431">

                <pb facs="0603" n="523" xml:id="pg523"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">561.35E1/110: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-01-10T20:00:00-05:00">January 10, 1936—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8. Your No. 4, January 3. Please inform the Foreign Office in response to their
                    informal note that this Government is sympathetic to and interested in the
                    general possibility of a new international sugar agreement. The uncertainty
                    recently created regarding governmental policy in the agricultural field<anchor
                        n="7" xml:id="d431-f1"/> makes it necessary, however, to postpone reply to
                    the British communication. As soon as this uncertainty is ended, we will
                    formulate our views.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d431-f1" n="7" xml:id="d431-f1-content">
                <p>By the invalidation of the Agricultural Adjustment Act (approved May 12, 1933; 48
                    Stat. 31) on January 6, 1936, by the Supreme Court; 297 U. S. 1.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
